---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import SwitchDemo from "../../components/SwitchDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../components/code-theme";

const usageExample = `import { Switch } from "@/components/ui/switch";

<label className="flex items-center gap-2">
  <Switch
    checked={wifi}
    onChange={(event) => setWifi(event.target.checked)}
    stateLabels={{ on: "On", off: "Off" }}
  />
  Wi-Fi
</label>`;
---

<ComponentPage slug="switch">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    It is a native <code>&lt;input type="checkbox"&gt;</code> with <code>role="switch"</code>, so
    Space toggles it and screen readers announce it as on or off.
  </Fragment>

  <SwitchDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Usage</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={usageExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>For settings that apply immediately.</strong> A switch turns something on or off
        right away, like a light switch. For a choice that only applies when a form is submitted,
        use a Checkbox.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Label it.</strong> Wrap it and its text in a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>, or put it
        in a Field. The label names the setting; do not change it with the state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>State is not color alone.</strong> The thumb moves from left to right when on. For a
        visible word as well, pass <code>stateLabels</code>; that text is hidden from screen
        readers, which already announce the state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Native state.</strong> The browser maps <code>checked</code> to the switch state, so
        there is no <code>aria-checked</code> attribute to keep in sync. Use <code>checked</code>{" "}
        and
        <code>onChange</code>, or <code>defaultChecked</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reduced motion.</strong> The thumb slides only when the user has not asked for
        reduced motion; otherwise it jumps. In forced-colors mode the thumb keeps a system color, so
        it stays visible.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/switch.tsx</code>, the same path as shadcn's Switch. Unlike
    shadcn's, it is a native input: <code>onChange</code> replaces <code>onCheckedChange</code>. It
    needs only your <code>cn()</code> helper; if your <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the
    current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing it, and answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
